The terminal bell no longer puts 0x07 bytes onto stdout in -p or piped mode. That's half 1 of #976, committed as c21b1fa6; build, fmt, clippy (-D warnings) and the full cargo test all pass.

Reproduced first. I ran the existing test armed_doors_keep_streamed_partial_answer_when_turn_dies_mid_stream unchanged. On plain -p, stdout ended in …PARTIAL_ANSWER_7Q\n\u{7}\u{7}: two bell bytes after six retries. I added two assertions to that test:
• stdout contains no 0x07 byte, on both armed doors (plain -p, and piped stdin without --print).
• stdout is non-empty and contains PARTIAL_ANSWER_7Q, so an empty buffer can't pass the byte check.

At HEAD the new assertion failed by name on [plain -p].

The fix is in src/format/mod.rs. A new pure helper, bell_destination(stdout_tty, stdout_reserved, stderr_tty), decides where the bell goes, and maybe_ring_bell just follows it:
• If stdout is a terminal and not reserved for the answer, the bell goes to stdout. That covers every interactive REPL user, so the REPL behaves exactly as before.
• Otherwise it goes to stderr if stderr is a terminal.
• If neither stream is a terminal, it isn't written at all.

A table test covers all 8 combinations, including the interactive row above. Before, the code only skipped the bell when stdout was reserved (--print / json); it never checked whether stdout was a terminal, so plain -p into a pipe still got the bytes.

Positive control. I made the helper always return stdout, marked NEUTERED, and restored the file in the same command. Both the #976 assertion and the table test went red by name. The restored file has no marker left.

• **Half 2 is still open:** the partial answer is still re-streamed once per retry (six copies in that test). It needs a design decision, so the existing `contains` assertion is unchanged and a comment in the test says only the bell half is pinned. #976 stays open.
• **CHANGELOG:** I added one line under the existing Unreleased section.
• **Docs:** the only bell mention in `docs/src` describes the REPL, which hasn't changed, so no doc edit was needed.
